optional; I'll include one since it exercises the function in its intended context and adds assertion value (issubclass behavior). But careful about the ABCMeta requirement: for `__subclasshook__` to be consulted by issubclass, the class must use ABCMeta as metaclass. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef test_check_methods_used_via_subclasshook():\n    import abc\n\n    class HasRead(metaclass=abc.ABCMeta):\n        @classmethod\n        def __subclasshook__(cls, other):\n            if cls is HasRead:\n                return _check_methods(other, 'read')\n            return NotImplemented\n\n    class Reader:\n        def read(self):\n            return 'data'\n\n    class NotAReader:\n        pass\n\n    class NullReader:\n        read = None\n\n    assert issubclass(Reader, HasRead)\n    assert not issubclass(NotAReader, HasRead)\n    assert not issubclass(NullReader, HasRead)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `issubclass(NullReader, HasRead)`: hook returns NotImplemented \u2192 ABCMeta falls back to normal check \u2192 NullReader is not a subclass of HasRead \u2192 False. Good. But careful: `not issubclass(...)` \u2014 issubclass returns a bool. With ABCMeta, `__subclasscheck__` returns True/False. Good.\n\nHmm, but does `abc.ABCMeta.__subclasshook__` get called for `issubclass(Reader, HasRead)`? Yes: ABCMeta.__subclasscheck__ calls `cls.__subclasshook__(subclass)`; our hook returns `_check_methods(Reader, 'read')` \u2192 True \u2192 issubclass True. \n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety: ABCMeta.__subclasscheck__ first checks `cls.__dict__.get('__subclasshook__')`? It does:\n\n```python\ndef __subclasscheck__(cls, subclass):\n    ...\n    # Check the subclass hook\n    ok = cls.__subclasshook__(subclass)\n    if ok is not NotImplemented:\n        assert ok in (True, False)\n        return ok\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually it checks `cls._abc_registry`, `cls._abc_cache`, then `cls.__subclasshook__(subclass)`. Our classmethod is found. Good.\n\nAlso note: in Python 3.x, `metaclass=abc.ABCMeta` works directly. pysnooper supports old Pythons via six, but the test env is presumably modern. Using `metaclass=abc.ABCMeta` in a class definition requires Python 3. Fine.\n\nShould I include this ABC test? It adds integration value. But it also risks environment issues (none really). I'll include it \u2014 it's correct.\n\nHmm, one thing: `assert not issubclass(NotAReader, HasRead)` \u2014 with ABCMeta, when hook returns NotImplemented, it falls back to the regular subclass check: NotAReader's MRO is (NotAReader, object); HasRead is not in it \u2192 False. Good.\n\nNow, cleanup/state pollution: All classes are defined inside test functions \u2192 no module-level pollution. No monkeypatching needed since `_check_methods` is pure. The instructions emphasize cleanup \"so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 here it's not appropriate since there's no global state touched. I'll note that implicitly by keeping everything local.\n\nTop-level code: just imports. No pytest.main.\n\nLet me also double check: does `from pysnooper.utils import _check_methods` risk ImportError due to circular imports? pysnooper's `__init__.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom .pytracer import Tracer, disable, enable\nfrom .variables import CommonVariable, Unexpanded, BaseVariable\n...\n```\n\nAnd pytracer imports variables, variables imports utils (probably). So by the time pysnooper.utils is imported (as part of the package import chain), everything is fine. And `from pysnooper.utils import _check_methods` triggers `import pysnooper` \u2192 `__init__` \u2192 pytracer \u2192 variables \u2192 utils. All good.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 does pysnooper.variables import from pysnooper.utils? Let me verify quickly with get_info(\"BaseVariable\") to be thorough... Actually the concern would only matter if utils.py imported variables.py. Let me check get_info on \"get_shortish_repr\" \u2014 that would show utils.py content around it, possibly revealing